**Alanine** is an \(\alpha\)-amino acid whose **R group is a methyl group** (\(\text{-CH}_3\)). Condensed formula: \(\text{CH}_3\text{-CH(NH}_2\text{)-COOH}\).

The diagram (Figure 9.1) shows the central **\(\alpha\)-carbon** with its four valencies occupied by:
1. a **carboxyl group** (\(\text{-COOH}\)), drawn above the carbon;
2. a **hydrogen** atom (\(\text{H}\)), drawn to the left;
3. an **amino group** (\(\text{-NH}_2\)), drawn to the right;
4. the **R group, methyl** (\(\text{-CH}_3\)), drawn below and boxed as the variable group.
